  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["C1"],
      "pins": ["C1.1", "C1.2"],
      "nets": ["Display_Power_Switch", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the EPD_Driver sheet, C1 (the bulk decoupling capacitor on the Display_Power_Switch / EPD VDD rail, in parallel with C2) is drawn as 0.1 uF / 16 V.",
      "expected": "The specification's EPD component-matching table explicitly assigns C1 to the 1 uF / 25 V group that corresponds to the PDI reference's C2 default decoupling cap ('C2 default: 1 µF/25 V | C1/C5/C7/C8/C9/C10 = 1 µF/25 V | Matches default'). All other members of that group (C5, C7, C8, C9, C10) match 1 uF/25 V on the drawing; only C1 deviates.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "C1 0.1uF/16V (EPD_Driver.kicad_sch, sheet /EPD_Driver/, left of J1, on the Display_Power_Switch node)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "C2 default: 1 µF/25 V | C1/C5/C7/C8/C9/C10 = 1 µF/25 V | Matches default"}
      ],
      "impact": "The EPD VDD rail loses its intended 1 uF bulk decoupling (reduced to 0.1 uF), so the display supply is under-decoupled during boost-converter/charge-pump switching; this can cause display refresh glitches or noise. The 16 V rating is still adequate for the 3.3 V rail, so the board is not damaged, but it no longer matches the reference decoupling network.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic otherwise matches the specification, but on the EPD_Driver sheet C1 is drawn as 0.1 uF/16 V whereas the spec's component table (and the five matching siblings C5/C7/C8/C9/C10) require C1 = 1 uF/25 V as the EPD VDD bulk decoupling cap; this single value deviation is the anomaly."
}

